WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.780
 The flying car is no longer a joke, China's EVTOL moment.

2
00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:10.280
 Yeheng received the world's first full-type certificate for a pilotless passenger EVTOL.

3
00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:15.540
 Tourists in Guangzhou are already buying tickets, the West is still writing regulations.

4
00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:21.660
 For over a century, the flying car has been the ultimate symbol of a promised future that never

5
00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:26.540
 arrived. But while the West has been debating zoning laws and writing theoretical regulations,

6
00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:29.760
 China has quietly kicked the door to the future wide open.

7
00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:35.060
 The flying car is no longer a rendering. It is a commercial reality and the West is dangerously

8
00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:37.940
 behind. The Regulatory Rubicon

9
00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:43.600
 Yeheng, a Chinese autonomous aerial vehicle technology company, recently achieved what

10
00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:48.160
 no Western company has managed. They received a full-type certificate for a pilotless,

11
00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:52.840
 passenger-carrying EVTOL aircraft from the Civil Aviation Administration of China.

12
00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:58.280
 This is not a testing permit or a research exemption. It is a standard airworthiness certificate.

13
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:03.640
 The EVTOL aircraft carries two passengers, has absolutely no flight controls in the cabin,

14
00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:09.800
 and uses 16 propellers mounted in coaxial pairs for redundancy. It flies at roughly 130 km

15
00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:14.700
 slash h. Ordinary tourists in Guangzhou are currently buying tickets, climbing into a pod

16
00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:18.060
 with zero controls, and taking real, commercial sightseeing flights.

17
00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:20.280
 The Supply Chain and the State

18
00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:24.520
 How did China cross this regulatory Rubicon while Western companies are still trapped in

19
00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:29.340
 certification purgatory? It comes down to two structural advantages. First, the supply chain,

20
00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:34.240
 China dominates battery technology, the single hardest component in any EVTOL. Second, state

21
00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:38.180
 mandate, Beijing has formally designated the low-altitude economy as a strategic emerging

22
00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:42.280
 industry, projecting the sector will reach 1.5 trillion yuan by 2025.

23
00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:44.680
 The Implication for the Watch

24
00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:50.940
 The true use case for EVTOLs is not replacing the Honda Civic in an American suburb. The use case

25
00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:55.860
 is deploying transportation networks in regions where roads are poor, congested, or non-existent.

26
00:01:55.860 --> 00:02:00.080
 Just as much of the developing world skipped landlines and went straight to mobile phones,

27
00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:04.940
 entire regions will skip building highways and go straight to low-altitude aerial networks.

28
00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:11.540
 The pattern is undeniable. The West invents the concept, gets bogged down in regulatory friction,

29
00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:13.280
 and deploys it at scale.
